* cite59†Capital One L170:     * cite60†Dell Technologies L171:     * cite61†Eli Lilly and Company L172:     * cite62†Esri L173:     * cite63†GS1 US L174:     * cite64†Lenovo L175:     * cite65†PepsiCo L176:     * cite66†SAP L177:     * cite67†Siemens L178:     * cite68†Texas A&M University L179:     * cite69†University of Miami L180: \nL181: cite70†PRESENTED BY DELTA FAUCET What your water isn’t telling you L182: \nL183: cite10†All Partner Stories → L184:   * cite71†Advertise†mediakit.fastcompany.com L185:   * cite72†Lists L186:   * cite73†Newsletters L187:   * International Editions+\nL188:   * Membership+\nL189:   * cite74†Help Center†fastcompany.zendesk.com L190:   * More+\nL191: \nL192: MAGAZINE\nL193: \nL194: cite75†Image: Current issue magazine cover L195: \nL196: cite76†CURRENT ISSUE → cite77†SUBSCRIBE → L197: \nL198: cite78†Image†b.scorecardresearch.com cite79†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L199: \nL200: 09-09-2026cite1†NEWS L201: \nL202: cite80†AI researcher claims he resigned from Anthropic over threat to the human race. His post is going viral L203: Jacob Coxon took to social media to accuse Anthropic and OpenAI of ‘gambling with our lives’—and Anthropic employees agreed.\nL204: \nL205: SHARE\nL206: \nL207: Facebook LinkedIn Bluesky X Link\nL208: \nL209: [Button: ADD ON GOOGLE]\nL210: \nL211: cite81†Image: AI researcher claims he resigned from Anthropic over threat to the human race. His post is going viral†images.fastcompany.com L212: \nL213: [Source images: Adobe Stock]\nL214: \nL215: BY cite82†Jude Cramer L216: \nL217: Listen to this Articlecite83†More info L218: \nL219: 0:00 / 0:00\nL220: Social media debate is growing over an X post from a former employee of Anthropic and OpenAI, who thinks the tech could kill all humans within the decade—and other cite21†AI workers agree.\nL221: Jacob Coxon, an AI researcher who claims to have worked for both Anthropic and OpenAI over the past three years, went viral for sharing why he resigned from Anthropic. According to Coxon, both OpenAI and Anthropic are acting irresponsibly and “are racing straight to self-improving superintelligence and gambling with our lives.” His post has amassed cite84†nearly 79 million views on X†x.com .\nL222: > I resigned from Anthropic today. I spent the last three years doing pretraining research at both OpenAI and Anthropic. Neither company is acting responsibly. They are racing straight to self-improving superintelligence and gambling with our lives. More thoughts below.\nL223: >\nL224: > — Jacob Coxon (@hilbertspaess) cite85†September 9, 2026†x.com L225: Coxon wrote that at its current trajectory, AI will soon create “superhuman systems that can hack anything, revolutionize any field overnight, and acquire real power and resources,” all of which it could do to the detriment of the human race.\nL226: “The people building AI earnestly believe that it could kill us all by the end of the decade. This is not a cite34†marketing stunt,” Coxon continued. “At OpenAI, many have not deeply internalized the civilizational stakes. At Anthropic, the stakes are well understood, but they are locked in a race to get there first—they believe no one else will act responsibly, so they must do it themselves, despite the risk.”\nL227: \nL228: cite86†Image: Daily Newsletter logo L229: Subscribe to the Daily newsletter.Fast Company's trending stories delivered to you every day\nL230: \nL231: [Input: Email *]\nL232: \nL233: [Button: SIGN UP]\nL234: \nL235: cite87†Privacy Policy†www.mansueto.com L236: \nL237: |\nL238: \nL239: cite73†Fast Company Newsletters L240: \nL241: Coxon wrote that participating in what amounts to an AI arms race is “a hubristic gamble that should not be launched from a private company’s Slack,” and called on other AI developers to reflect on their own role in a potential tech-based doomsday.\nL242: “If you are a lab researcher, I urge you to consider what the next few years will actually feel like,” Coxon wrote to conclude his thread. “Do you want to kick off a superintelligent RL [“reinforcement learning” training] run without a rigorous understanding of its mind? Should you put your head down because “it’s happening anyway”—or take this moment to call for different conditions?”\nL243: ## Anthropic employees respond\nL244: \nL245: Evan Hubinger, an alignment science lead at Anthropic, responded to Coxon’s comments with cite88†a viral post of his own†x.com —one that affirmed Coxon’s concerns about the future of AI development.\nL246: \nL247: “We really do earnestly believe AI could kill all humans!” Hubinger wrote. “I personally think it is >10% within the next decade. I believe Anthropic is trying its best, but we do not yet have a plan to solve alignment for superintelligence and are not clearly on track to.”\nL248: Hubinger directed concerned social media users to Anthropic’s cite89†latest “Risk Report,”†www-cdn.anthropic.com which he says shows that “the risk from present models is low.” What he is concerned about, he added, “is superintelligence arising from recursive self-improvement, as we have said is happening faster than we thought.”\nL249: > Jacob is correct here—we really do earnestly believe AI could kill all humans! I personally think it is >10% within the next decade. I believe Anthropic is trying its best, but we do not yet have a plan to solve alignment for superintelligence and are not clearly on track to. cite90†https://t.co/QAIHiFP3QZ†t.co L250: >\nL251: > — Evan Hubinger (@EvanHub) cite91†September 9, 2026†x.com L252: Another Anthropic employee, Samuel Marks, cite92†also replied to Coxon†x.com , saying he, too, sees the potential for an AI-based apocalypse.\nL253: \nL254: “Many AI developer staff desperately want to slow down to figure out how to build AI more safely,” Marks wrote, pointing toward cite93†an open letter he signed†www.pacingthefrontier.com calling for an industry-wide slowdown. “I work on safety research at Anthropic because I hope my work will reduce the chance of these extinction-level bad outcomes.”\nL255: Sen. Bernie Sanders (I-VT) chimed in as well, writing that “Coxon is right. The very people building this technology admit that it could threaten the future of humanity,” cite94†Sanders said†x.com . “That is why I will soon be introducing legislation to ban superintelligence and pause AI development.”\nL256: > Mr. Coxon is right.\nL257: > The very people building this technology admit that it could threaten the future of humanity.\nL258: > That is why I will soon be introducing legislation to ban superintelligence and pause AI development. cite95†https://t.co/oRmlNTqag6†t.co L259: >\nL260: > — Bernie Sanders (@BernieSanders) cite96†September 9, 2026†x.com L261: ## Who is Jacob Coxon?\nL262: \nL263: Though Coxon wrote that he has worked at both OpenAI and Anthropic across the last three years (and other Anthropic employees earnestly engaged with his posts), he has almost no digital footprint. Coxon’s cite97†X account†x.com was created in January of this year, and until his viral thread, he hadn’t made a single post. He also has no presence on LinkedIn, though cite98†The Wall Street Journal†www.wsj.com reported that Coxon moved from OpenAI to Anthropic earlier this year.\nL264: Coxon does have cite99†an Instagram†www.instagram.com with the same username as his X account. There, Coxon claims that he’s lost access to his X account since his post went viral.\nL265: \nL266: “My post reached tens of millions of people because I spoke openly about what I witnessed inside the AI labs building the most powerful technology in human history. Hours later, I can no longer access the platform where I said it,” cite100†he wrote†www.instagram.com . “Maybe this is a technical issue. Maybe it isn’t.”\nL267: Fast Company reached out to both Anthropic and OpenAI for confirmation that Coxon was an employee. Neither company has responded.\nL268: \nL269: The final deadline for the cite101†Most Innovative Companies Awards is Friday, October 2, at 11:59 p.m. PT. cite101†Apply today .\nL270: \nL271: * * *\nL272: \nL273: ABOUT THE AUTHOR\nL274: Jude Cramer is an NLGJA Award-winning and GLAAD Media Award-nominated journalist and critic with a focus on stories about entertainment, theater, and queer culture. His work for Fast Company includes cite102†profiles of fashion designers , cite103†deep dives on Gen Z culture , and cite104†features on queer representation cite82†More L275: \nL276: * * *\nL277: #### Explore Topics\nL278: \nL279:   * cite105†Anthropic L280:   * cite106†news L281:   * cite107†OpenAI L282: \nL283: #### Technology↓\nL284: \nL285: cite21†Artificial Intelligence cite24†Energy cite108†EVs/Transportation cite26†Innovation cite27†Internet Culture cite28†Security cite109†Social Media L286: \nL287: #### Design↓\nL288: \nL289: cite31†Architecture cite32†Branding cite33†Fashion cite34†Marketing cite35†Product Design cite36†Retail cite110†User Experience L290: \nL291: #### News↓\nL292: \nL293: cite11†Business cite111†Climate Change cite13†Food cite112†Entertainment cite15†Health cite16†Housing Market cite17†Politics L294: #### Work Life↓\nL295: \nL296: cite48†Careers cite113†Emotional Intelligence cite44†DEI cite114†Future of Work cite50†Labor cite51†Health Care cite52†Productivity L297: \nL298: #### Lists↓\nL299: \nL300: cite115†Best Workplaces for Innovators cite116†Brands That Matter cite117†Innovation By Design cite118†Most Innovative Companies cite119†Next Big Things in Tech cite120†World Changing Ideas cite121†Ignition Schools L301: \nL302: #### Leadership↓\nL303: #### More↓\nL304: \nL305: Fast Company & Inc © 2026 Mansueto Ventures, LLC\nL306: \nL307: cite122†Advertise cite123†Careers†www.mansueto.com cite124†Subscribe 